# Ruflo orchestration (Cursor)

Ruflo is an optional coordination layer for this project. It does not replace Cursor.

Keep using normal Cursor plugins, skills, MCPs, rules, and tools. The main Cursor agent owns final integration and validation.

Do not create swarms for trivial work. Do not re-run `ruflo-cursor-on` when Ruflo is already ON and MCP tools are loaded in this chat — just use it. Leave Ruflo ON after the task unless the user explicitly asks to disable it.

If this chat has no Ruflo MCP tools after enable, stop and tell the user to open a new Agent chat. Do not pretend the tools exist.

For work the user asked Ruflo to handle, Ruflo may decompose tasks, route subtasks, coordinate workers, reuse `.ruflo/state` memory, and return results for you to integrate.

Never treat Ruflo registry tools such as `agent_spawn` / `agent_execute` as real executing workers. Those are registry records, not Cursor processes.

Real delegated work must use Cursor Agent CLI workers via `.ruflo/cursor/bin/adapter.mjs` (`agent -p --model ...`). Never `codex exec`. Use verified Cursor routing in `.ruflo/cursor/model-routing.json`. Check `.ruflo/cursor/metrics/` for the actual model, PID, and result. Parallel workers only when useful.

Maximum concurrency is 3. Concurrent writers need separate Git worktrees or distinct directories. Codex Ruflo commands, `~/.codex`, and global Cursor MCP stay untouched.
